ial-position effect). One shared parameter, `kappa`, sets how quickly that weight falls off moving inward from each edge. Its prior is centred at zero, so the data can also make the middle count more. The model has no heads/tails balance term and no lapse, because this is an exploratory slot and adding either would be grafting.\n\n**Where it disagrees with the best model:** on pairs with matched overall switching rate and heads/tails balance that differ only in where the repeats sit. The best model, `lapse_individual_balance_alternation`, predicts indifference there. This model predicts that a streak at an edge makes a sequence look less random than the same streak in the middle. It targets the strongest critique result, `end_streak_pref_matched_alt`: observed 0.608 vs predicted 0.529, p = 0.03. That result did not survive the false-discovery-rate correction (q = 0.12), so it is a lead rather than an established gap.\n\n**Self-check result:** the short single-chain fit gave ELPD-LOO -1001.2. That is ahead of the other alternation-only models (about -1009) but 49 nats behind the best (-952.4). Treat the number as rough: 3.4% of trials had Pareto k > 0.5, above the 1% tolerance, so this estimate is flagged unreliable. A single chain also can't report R-hat, so convergence is unverified.\n\n**Not yet checked:** whether it converges in admission's full fit, and whether it passes the novelty gate. If the fitted `kappa` comes out near zero, it predicts almost the same as `personal_ideal_alternation` and would be rejected as a near-duplicate.","ttft_ms":10514,"type":"result","duration_ms":492890,"uuid":"ccf9897b-c95f-40af-873f-a3235e34d6fb","ttft_stream_ms":3653,"time_to_request_ms":141,"first_content_frame_ms":3653,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
